## 2026-09-02 — .49 IS PRODUCTION, .47 IS STAGING (user, authoritative)

**Never add load to `.49` (192.168.1.49, ai-server, 24 cores).** It runs a
real production service stack — measured 2026-09-02: 1,402 processes,
`mongod` 3.75 GB resident, `java`, four `ruby`, `devpi-server`, `uvicorn`,
taking 23–30% user CPU continuously on their own. Our stable-half sweep was
taking a further **~8.8 of 24 cores** (14 workers at 50–99%).

**`.47` (claw-47, tararis-ai3, 12 cores) is staging — heavy load is fine.**
Prefer it for everything, and give it the memory-hungry passes too: it had
17 GB available against `.49`'s 7 GB, and a GP cell needs ~1.6 GB.

Corollary: the reallocation instinct "move work to the idle machine" is
WRONG here whenever the idle machine is `.49`. Idle capacity on `.49`
belongs to production, not to us.

### Swap on .49 is a non-issue (settled 2026-09-02)
Swap showed 8127/8191 MB full, but `vmstat` si/so = **0/0** — full but
static, i.e. idle daemon pages the kernel parked, not thrashing. Our share
was 2,096 MB of 8,582 MB (24%); the rest is the service stack (devpi 1 GB,
java 876 MB, four ruby ~1.5 GB, mongod 409 MB). Do not diagnose "swap full"
as a sweep problem again — measure si/so, not usage.

### Reading sweep liveness correctly
`ps ... | grep run_regime` matches only the PARENT processes, which sit at
0.0% CPU in `futex_do_wait` while their pool works — that is HEALTHY, not a
stall. The workers are the children: `pgrep -P <parent>`. Misreading this
once produced a false "the sweep is stalled" conclusion.
